  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nLingering showers and thunderstorms continue to make their way \neast this morning, with the main impacts being gusty winds in the \n40 to 50 mph range.\n\nModels are depicting heavy smoke settling across the region \ntoday, with reductions to visibility depicted. Areas of smoke have\nbeen introduced to the forecast. This could limit maximum daytime\nheating and diminish the lowest extent of minimum RH values. Take\nnecessary precautions if you have vulnerabilities to smoke.\n\nHot and mostly dry conditions persist the rest of today, with \nhigh temperatures in the upper 90s and low 100sF. Heat Advisories \ncontinue across southern Montana and portions of northern Wyoming \nthrough this evening. Extreme Heat Warnings continue through \nMonday evening from for counties east of and including Rosebud.\n\nAn additional wave will bring showers and thunderstorms to the \nregion this afternoon and evening. Storms do not look to be all \ntoo strong, though inverted-V soundings indicate the chance for \nstrong and erratic wind gusts and brief heavy rainfall. Minimum \nrelative humidity values will be low, in the teens and 20s \npercent. Look for widespread elevated fire weather conditions, \nthough weaker winds will help mitigate worse fire weather \nconditions.\n\nRidging largely continues through much of the week, with highs in\nthe 90s to low 100sF. There looks to be low chances (10-40 \npercent) for afternoon showers and thunderstorms through the week,\nwith the main concerns being gusty winds and brief heavy rain. \nPWAT values remain above normal through at least Wednesday, \n100-150% above normal before dropping next weekend.\n\nA pattern change may come about early next week, as a few models \ndepict a potential ridge crasher. This could finally bring high \ntemperatures down towards normals, in the 80sF. Continue to \nmonitor the forecast as we progress through the week.\n\nMatos",
